  "question": {
    "text": "<p>The incorrect statements from the following is:<br />A.&nbsp;The electrical work that a reaction can perform at constant pressure and temperature is equal to the reaction Gibbs energy.<br />B.&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi><mi>cell</mi><mo>&#8728;</mo></msubsup></math>&nbsp; is dependent on the pressure.<br />C.&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msubsup><mi>dE</mi><mi>cell</mi><mo>&#176;</mo></msubsup><mi>dT</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#916;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></msub><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mo>&#176;</mo></msup></mrow><mi>nF</mi></mfrac></math><br />D. A cell is operating reversibly if the cell potential is exactly balanced by an opposing source of potential difference.</p>",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [],
  "correctValue": "1",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi><mi>cell</mi><mo>&#8728;</mo></msubsup></math>&nbsp;is dependent on the equilibrium constant, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></math>,&nbsp;which is&nbsp;not dependent on the pressure of any gas involved in the cell.&nbsp;Relation between Electrochemical Cell Potential and Gibbs free Energy change is given by; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#916;</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>-</mo><mi>nFE</mi></math> where Delta G is Gibbs Free energy change , n is the number of electrons involved in the reaction and F is Faraday and E is the Cell potential.</p><p>We know that,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>dG</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>VdP</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>SdT</mi></math></p><p>At constant pressure&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>dP</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi>dG</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mi>SdT</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mi>dG</mi><mi>dT</mi></mfrac><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"></mspace><mo>&#8710;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><msub><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mo>&#8710;</mo><mfenced><mi>dG</mi></mfenced></mrow><mi>dT</mi></mfrac></mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></msub><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"></mspace></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>dE</mi><mi>cell</mi><mo>&#176;</mo></msubsup><mi>dT</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>&#8710;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi></mrow><mi>nF</mi></mfrac></math></p><p>A cell is operating reversibly if the cell potential is exactly balanced by an opposing source of potential difference.</p></blockquote>",
    "image": null
  },
